{
  "id": "grammar.a1.38.a",
  "title": "People in the plural",
  "sections": [
    {
      "type": "text",
      "title": "Explanation",
      "content": "People words follow the same pattern: *-k*, with a linking vowel after a consonant. *gyerek* → *gyerekek*, *ember* → *emberek*, *barát* → *barátok*, *család* → *családok*. Words that end in a vowel just add *-k*: *nő* → *nők*, *fiú* → *fiúk*. One to learn as it comes: *férfi* → *férfiak*."
    },
    {
      "type": "table",
      "title": "People words",
      "rows": [
        [
          "gyerek",
          "gyerekek (children)"
        ],
        [
          "ember",
          "emberek (people)"
        ],
        [
          "barát",
          "barátok (friends)"
        ],
        [
          "család",
          "családok (families)"
        ],
        [
          "lány",
          "lányok (girls)"
        ],
        [
          "nő",
          "nők (women)"
        ],
        [
          "fiú",
          "fiúk (boys)"
        ],
        [
          "férfi",
          "férfiak (men)"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "examples",
      "title": "Examples",
      "items": [
        {
          "spanish": "A gyerekek itt vannak.",
          "english": "The children are here."
        },
        {
          "spanish": "Az emberek együtt vannak.",
          "english": "The people are together."
        },
        {
          "spanish": "A családok ott vannak.",
          "english": "The families are there."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "tip",
      "content": "Even though *gyerek* already ends in *-k*, the plural does not merge with it: *gyerek* + *ek* = *gyerekek*. The ending attaches to the whole word."
    }
  ]
}
